#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""ccfleet -- run headless Claude Code sessions across a fleet of repos, each
one billed to its own repo, started only while the machine has headroom.

Why it exists: per-repo cost tools (`ccrepo`) label usage by the session's
working-directory basename. A subagent started with the Agent tool runs in its
parent's directory, so work done "for" another repo by an Agent subagent bills
the orchestrator's repo, not the repo it was for. The fix is structural: each
repo's work runs as its own `claude -p` session started INSIDE a worktree of
that repo whose basename is the repo name. Doing that by hand for a dozen repos,
without crashing the machine, is what this instrument does.

THE LESSONS IT CARRIES (each one paid for in a real run):
  * `claude -p` is always started with stdin from /dev/null. Without it a
    "no stdin" warning lands in the JSON log and the result cannot be parsed.
  * Every launch names its model (`--model`). A launch without one silently
    inherits the default tier; tools/modelguard.py blocks such launches.
  * The worktree's basename is the repo name, under a per-job parent directory
    (`<worktrees_root>/<label>-<HHMM>/<repo>`). That basename is the billing key.
  * Never repo work as an Agent subagent of the orchestrator (see above).
  * A public repo's prompt never names a private repo. Rendering refuses to
    launch a prompt for a public (or unknown-visibility) repo that contains any
    configured private name wherever no letter or digit touches it, so a
    <label>-<HHMM> folder, a name-kit folder, name_notes and atelier--name are
    caught. The guard is a literal match, so it catches the names you list and
    nothing you forgot to list. It reads the user-level instructions file each
    launch appends too: a public or unknown repo's launch is refused when that
    file names one (instructions_guard "warn": a line per name, and it goes on).
    That file's @-imports are inlined for a private repo only; a public or
    unknown one gets each import (on a line of its own or inside one) replaced
    by a note that names no path, which is what the guard reads, and its
    settings deny Read of every file the imports resolve to, so a private
    name kept in an imported file reaches no public session by what ccfleet
    appends, and the appended text does not say where that file is (PK5,
    DP18). Its settings also switch the repo's auto memory off: Claude Code
    loads that index into every session whatever ccfleet appends (DP4).
  * This is Python, not zsh, on purpose: zsh reads a script as it runs, so
    editing a driver script mid-run broke every running job's tail. Python
    compiles the whole file at start, so a running driver is immune; a job
    started after an edit runs the edited copy.
  * Start one job at a time, staggered, and only while load, free memory and
    free disk all have headroom: parallel test runs inside headless sessions
    have taken a 16 GB laptop past load 100. Workers run their own tests
    niced and single-process; say so in the prompt.
  * An end is clean only when the session exited 0 and its JSON flags no
    error; anything else reads as ERROR (TIMEOUT at the time limit) whatever
    its text says, so a cut-off pass is never read as DONE. A worktree is
    removed only after a clean RESULT, never by force, and never while it
    holds uncommitted files or commits on no remote branch. The drain stops
    after three unclean ends in a row, which is how a spent budget shows up
    from outside.
  * A new session is given its id up front (--session-id), so a session
    killed before it prints still has one on its START line and its claim.
  * A session still running at session_timeout_minutes (default 480) is
    ended; a stopping driver reports a hung child instead of waiting forever.
  * Every session runs with CLAUDE_CODE_PRINT_BG_WAIT_CEILING_MS=0. Without
    it `claude -p` kills a background subagent about ten minutes after the
    orchestrator's last turn, so a reviewer working on act 2 died mid-act.
  * `claude -p` prints only the last turn. A session whose last turn followed
    a background notification printed no RESULT line though its report was
    written; the harvest then reads the session's own transcript, on a clean
    end only, taking the newest text that has a RESULT line and an ITEM: or
    REPO: line (the final-output shape), never a line of narration. The
    verdict is the RESULT line that stands in that shape: directly above the
    ITEM: or REPO: line (the review prompts), or the last line of a block that
    holds one (the fleet prompts); any other RESULT line is no verdict. A
    mention inside NOTES once read DONE (RK3), and an earlier mention could
    (PK4). A closing code fence is not a last line, and with one RESULT line
    in the text, blank lines may stand inside the shape (DP2); a clean word
    stands only when no other RESULT line names an unclean one (DP6). The
    END line's result_line= says in-shape, out-of-shape or none, and a drain
    counts an out-of-shape end apart, never as a bad end.
  * A `touch <kit_dir>/stop` ends launching cleanly; running jobs finish. A
    stop file older than the driver is ignored (and logged): last week's file
    once ended a new run before it started anything.
  * The ceiling is live: <kit_dir>/max holds it while a driver runs; edit the
    number to pace a run without stopping it.
  * One driver (drive or drain) per kit_dir: a second is refused while the
    first lives (flock), and children a dead driver started still hold slots.
  * A dry drain writes its own launched list and calls no queue.ranked, so a
    rehearsal never makes the real drain read a full queue as empty. A pick
    that ends before its session starts is taken back off the list.
  * Children start in their own session, so closing the driver's terminal
    does not end the sessions it started. A SIGTERM or SIGHUP to a launcher
    leaves its session running (it may hold unsaved work) and its process id
    recorded in <kit_dir>/sessions/, with its worktree and its settings
    copies; a resume, and any launch into that worktree (a fold), is refused
    while it runs (RK4, PK2), and its copies stay until it ends (PK1).
  * The config is the whole grant. A headless session would load the
    operator's user-level settings, whose allow rules widened a config that
    narrowed the tool list (a config without python3 ran python3). Every
    launch passes --setting-sources project,local and a --settings file
    ccfleet writes: the user-level settings less their allow rules, default
    mode and extra directories, and less any hook that prints an allow
    decision; their deny and ask rules, guard hooks, plugins and env are
    kept. Leaving the user source out also leaves out the user-level
    CLAUDE.md, so ccfleet appends it to the system prompt. A repo's own
    settings files still load; a launch prints a WIDENED line for each grant.
    What still widens it (`check` names each): those repo files, a kept hook
    whose script approves, kept keys such as enabledPlugins, env and the MCP
    approvals, and managed settings. A config that grants nothing runs in the
    default mode, where every call that would ask is refused; `check` says so.
    The two per-session copies (<kit_dir>/settings/) are removed when the
    ccfleet process that launched the session exits, unless a signal left
    the session running: then the next launch sweeps them once no live
    session record holds them (PK1). Every session's settings deny Read of
    that folder and of <kit_dir>/sessions/ (DP3).
  * A clean RESULT that leaves unlanded work is not clean: it reads as
    UNLANDED, the claim stays and the worktree is kept for a resume. A DONE
    that left uncommitted work once released its claim, tying the work to no
    queue item.

Usage:
  ccfleet check [--prompt FILE ...] [--set K=V ...]
                                      validate the config; name placeholders nothing fills
  ccfleet gate                        print the headroom reading; exit 0 if a start may go now
  ccfleet run REPO --prompt FILE [--label L] [--model M] [--set K=V ...] [--keep]
                                      [--in WT [--item ITEM]] [--dry-run]
  ccfleet drive JOBS [--max N] [--serial-per-repo] [--day YYYY-MM-DD] [--dry-run]
  ccfleet drain [--max N] [--dry-run]
  ccfleet plan [--json] [--fetch]               read the queue and the cost history; starts nothing
  ccfleet pass REPO ITEM BRIEF [--dry-run]      one drain pass (drain starts these)
  ccfleet resume REPO WORKTREE SESSION --prompt FILE [--item ITEM] [--set K=V ...]
  common: --config PATH (else $CCFLEET_CONFIG)  --by ID (else $CCFLEET_BY): the
  orchestrating session's id, filled into prompts as {BY}.

JOBS is a TSV file, one job per line: repo, label, prompt template, then any
number of KEY=VALUE fields that fill {KEY} in that template (MODEL=<id> sets the
model; a value written @file:PATH takes that file's text, for a multi-line
task). '#' lines and blank lines are skipped. Every label must be its own: a
file whose labels repeat is refused before anything starts, since the label
keys the result file. A job whose result file for the drive's day records a
clean RESULT, or a settled PARTIAL or BLOCKED, is skipped, so re-running a
drive resumes it (--day names the day of a drive begun before midnight). A
drive starts nothing once reset_utc is reached.

CONFIG is JSON (see instruments/ccfleet.example.json, every value fictional):
  kit_dir         logs/, results/<date>/, scratch/<repo>/, the stop file   (required)
  repos_root      primary checkouts live at <repos_root>/<repo>          (required)
  worktrees_root  job worktrees go under here                            (required)
  default_model   the model a job runs on when neither job nor repo names one (required)
  claude_bin      default "claude";   claude_args  extra args, e.g. a permission mode
                  (the narrowest that does the work: `check` names any set; never
                  --setting-sources, --settings or --append-system-prompt[-file])
  settings        Claude Code settings every session gets on top of the user-level
                  ones less their grants: with claude_args, the whole grant
  drop_hooks      texts: a user-level hook whose command holds one is left out
  session_timeout_minutes  default 480; 0 for no limit
  nice            default 5;  max_concurrent  default 4;  stagger_seconds  default 90;  poll_seconds 30
  gate            {max_load (default 2 x cores), min_mem_pct 25, min_disk_gb 20}
  reset_utc       "YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM": no new start at or after it (a budget reset);
                  a resume, and a fold of a pass that started (its result file
                  or a drain's START line for <repo>-<item>), still run
  private_names   names a public repo's prompt must never contain
  instructions_guard  "refuse" (default) or "warn": a public or unknown repo's launch
                  when the user-level CLAUDE.md it appends names a private name
  vars            template variables for every job (the principal's words, paths)
  env             extra environment variables for every session (NAME: value)
                  (a value starting ~/ in vars or env is expanded to the home folder)
  repos           {name: {model, notes, visibility, github, branch}}  per-repo facts
                  (branch: the mainline, read only for a repo with no remote)
  queue           drain: {next, ranked, take, release[, ended]} argv lists; plan: {evidence[, owed]};
                  and release_on / stop_on lists of RESULT words (see below)
  pass_prompt     drain only: the per-pass orchestrator prompt template
  pass_model      drain only: the orchestrator's model (the reviewer's tier is the prompt's business)

THE QUEUE INTERFACE (drain). The review queue itself is the adopter's own tool;
ccfleet only calls it. Each entry is an argv list, its placeholders filled
before the call: ranked gets {BY}; next {LAUNCHED} {BY}; evidence and owed
{FETCH}; take every prompt value and {ITEM} {BRIEF} {LOG} {SESSION}; release
and ended every prompt value and {ITEM} {BRIEF} {SESSION} {RESULT} {LOG}.
  Each is given QUEUE_TIMEOUT (600) seconds; one that cannot start or overruns
  counts as a failed call.
  next     prints "REPO ITEM BRIEF_RELPATH" for the next pass to run, or nothing
           when the queue is empty; tab-separated when a field may hold a
           space. It is called with the launched list's path in {LAUNCHED}
           (one tab-separated "REPO ITEM BRIEF" per line) so it can skip them.
  ranked   exits 0 only when the whole queue, every repo's, has been ranked
           and routed for this run (the review run skill's first step);
           called once before the first pick, with {BY}, after the reset
           check and never by a dry drain. Any other exit starts nothing: the
           drain prints "DRAIN EXIT: not ranked" and exits 1.
  take     claims the pass, with {SESSION} (the id the session will have);
           a nonzero exit means someone else holds it (skip).
  release  frees the claim after a pass ends with a RESULT in release_on
           (default DONE, SKIPPED) and nothing unlanded (else UNLANDED: no
           release); a refusal is printed, keeps the worktree and exits 2
           (pass, resume, run --item).
  ended    optional: called after every pass, resume or run --item, any
           RESULT, with {SESSION} {RESULT} {LOG} {WT} filled (to record them).
  unseal   optional: called once when a real drain that passed ranked ends,
           with {BY}, so the queue can end this run's seal (a restarted drain
           then plans and seals afresh): on its own, on a ^C, or on a SIGTERM
           or SIGHUP. A SIGKILL skips it; the seal then holds until it lapses,
           and a restart under the same --by runs on that earlier ranking. A
           failure is logged, never fatal.
  stop_on  RESULT words that stop the drain (default BUDGET EXHAUSTED).

THE SEAL. ccfleet never makes or checks a seal; it calls ranked once and
trusts its exit. What stops an unplanned paid pass is the adopter's queue, by
this contract. A run's ranking is sealed by the queue's own seal command,
which `/atelier:review run` calls with this run's plan, keyed by the run's
{BY}, and which refuses while any ready review is missing from the ranking,
unreasoned, on a closed route, on a later route than the best open one it
fits without saying why, or while two budgets are open and either lapse
time is unknown. ranked, next and take each exit nonzero (next printing
nothing) until this run's ranking is sealed, so a pass started by hand
(`ccfleet pass`, whose take carries its own --by) meets the same gate as a
drain's. next offers only what the sealed ranking routes to the local route
(every pass ccfleet launches is a local session; ccfleet passes no route
itself: the config's queue.next does, as the example's --route local, and
`check` says QUEUE ROUTE when it names none), in rank order, then the
stated spill: another route's review not yet started, but never from a budget
that lapses before the local one (that budget keeps its reviews) and never one
the seal marks not spillable; a review briefed after the seal runs last
(skills/review/run.md, the route rule). A seal lasts one run and no longer than the first
lapse among the budgets it routes to; where queue.unseal is set, the drain
calls it as it ends, which is how a queue sees the run's end. The seal gates
only the launches that call the queue: a drain's passes and `ccfleet pass`.
It does not gate `run --prompt` with the pass prompt and --set values (no
queue command; a fold calls only release), nor `resume`, which calls no
queue command before it starts. Whether a session that wrote a brief
may then drain it is the adopter's queue's to refuse (the reference queue
refuses to seal it) and otherwise rests on that session's own account (the
pass prompt's PROVENANCE). A reference queue that keeps this contract, which
the tests drive, ships as instruments/fixtures/review_queue_reference.py
(every name in it fictional).
  evidence plan only (and a dry drain): prints a JSON array, one object per
           briefed, unrun review, each with at least repo, item, brief, title;
           every other field (say briefed, his_words, dates_ahead, signals,
           live_write, fits (the routes it suits), lane) is passed through
           untouched. READ-ONLY: it must claim, seal and start nothing. An
           argument written exactly {FETCH} is filled with --fetch when
           `plan --fetch` is given and dropped otherwise, so the queue's own
           tool is the one that fetches; with no {FETCH} in the entry, --fetch
           fetches nothing and plan says so.
  owed     optional, plan only: prints a JSON array of owed reviews that have
           no brief yet: repo, item, title and optionally why. Read-only, and
           {FETCH} works here too. Without it plan prints owed as null.

THE PLAN (`ccfleet plan`). ccfleet reads and never judges: plan calls
queue.evidence (and queue.owed), reads its own past pass logs for measured cost,
and prints both (a table, or --json: evidence, owed, history, notes). Ranking
and routing are an agent's judgement, by `/atelier:review plan`, and the seal is
the adopter's queue's. plan calls none of ranked, take, next, release or ended,
and writes no launched list, claim, worktree or session. It is the one reading
of the queue: `/atelier:review plan` and `/atelier:review run` (at its start)
run it, and `drain --dry-run` prints the same reading, each line prefixed
`DRY plan:`, before its rehearsed launches (a queue with no evidence, or one
that fails, is said and the rehearsal carries on). Launches are rehearsed only
once this run's ranking is sealed: before it the dry drain prints "DRY not
sealed: no launches rehearsed" and exits 0; after it, each pick as "DRY
rehearsed launch: REPO ITEM BRIEF". The queue's "not sealed"
refusal is its own exit code, 3; any other failure of next, or a 3 after a
pick, is a queue error, exit 1. A failing queue command, or
one that prints something other than the JSON above, exits 1; a config with no
queue.evidence exits 64.
  Cost history: from <kit_dir>/logs/pass-*.out, the output of each pass a drain
  launched, the `claude -p --output-format json` log each names, and the logs of
  any resume and fold that finished it: for each completed pass (clean in one
  session, or finished clean by a resume or fold), the sum over its sessions of
  total_cost_usd, duration_ms and num_turns; the count, median and max of those,
  pooled, per repo and per model (modelUsage's costUSD: a reviewer subagent on
  its own model is that model's share, since total_cost_usd holds subagents
  too), with the subagents spawned and the cost basis. The CLI prices at list
  price; on a subscription plan the figure is notional, not money spent. No
  size is measured, so no figure is per size. Every pass ccfleet launches is a
  local session, so only the `local` route is measured; any other route a
  row's `fits` names reads "no measurement", as does local with no logs. A pass
  run by hand leaves no .out and is not counted, nor is one whose launcher a
  signal stopped (no END line). A later session's verdict is its own END
  line, kept as <log>.end; a log without one is read with exit 0 assumed.
  Log names, a drain's pass-*.out included, carry the date and second, so
  no later launch reopens one.
  Nothing is estimated.

Template variables always filled: {REPO} {GH} {BR} {VIS} {PIN} {WT} {PRIMARY}
{LANDING} {NOTES} {SCRATCH} {BY} {DATE} {SESSION}, plus config `vars`, plus the
job's own. `ccfleet check` names any placeholder of pass_prompt (or a --prompt)
that nothing fills.
{PIN}, when the template uses it and nothing supplies it, is the repo's atelier
pin (tools/pins.py, else the CLAUDE.md atelier@<sha>), else UNRESOLVED.
A pass adds {ITEM} {BRIEF} {REVIEW_LOG} (<kit_dir>/review-logs/<repo>-<item>-<hash>.md,
the reviewer's private log; the same path for its resume and fold; a log an
earlier pass left there is moved, dated, to review-logs/withdrawn/ first); a
resume adds {ITEM} and, with --item, {REVIEW_LOG}; run --in WT --item ITEM (a
fold) adds {ITEM} and {REVIEW_LOG} too.
A {UPPER_CASE} placeholder left unfilled is a refusal, not a silent gap.

Exit codes: 0 ran (the session's own verdict is in its END line and result
file) · 1 setup failed (or, for drain: not ranked, the queue's next failed, or
every pick ended before its session started; for plan: a queue command failed
or printed what it should not) · 2 claim refused (another
session holds it, another resume of that session runs, or the session to
resume, or one recorded in the worktree to launch into, still runs) or a
release refused
(pass, resume, run --item) · 3 reset reached · 4 queue empty (a drain that
started nothing) · 5 refused by the private-name guard or an unfilled
placeholder (check: a template has one) · 64 usage (incl. an unreadable or
malformed config, a repeated jobs label, --set with no '=', a repo given as a
path, a fold whose template is pass_prompt). See `man ccfleet`, THREATS, for
what can steer an unattended session.
Runtime: Python 3.8 or later (tested on 3.9 and 3.14 only), standard library
only; git, nice and ps on PATH, and memory_pressure on macOS (the gate's
memory reading); gh optional (visibility lookup when a repo's config does not
state it).
"""
